     1	# TUI plugins
     2	
     3	Technical reference for the current TUI plugin system.
     4	
     5	## Overview
     6	
     7	- TUI plugin config lives in `tui.json`.
     8	- Author package entrypoint is `@opencode-ai/plugin/tui`.
     9	- Internal plugins load inside the CLI app the same way external TUI plugins do.
    10	- Package plugins can be installed from CLI or TUI.
    11	- v1 plugin modules are target-exclusive: a module can export `server` or `tui`, never both.
    12	- Server runtime keeps v0 legacy fallback (function exports / enumerated exports) after v1 parsing.
    13	
    14	## TUI config
    15	
    16	Example:
    17	
    18	```json
    19	{
    20	  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/tui.json",
    21	  "theme": "smoke-theme",
    22	  "plugin": ["@acme/opencode-plugin@1.2.3", ["./plugins/demo.tsx", { "label": "demo" }]],
    23	  "plugin_enabled": {
    24	    "acme.demo": false
    25	  }
    26	}
    27	```
    28	
    29	- `plugin` entries can be either a string spec or `[spec, options]`.
    30	- Plugin specs can be npm specs, `file://` URLs, relative paths, or absolute paths.
    31	- Relative path specs are resolved relative to the config file that declared them.
    32	- A file module listed in `tui.json` must be a TUI module (`default export { id?, tui }`) and must not export `server`.
    33	- Duplicate npm plugins are deduped by package name; higher-precedence config wins.
    34	- Duplicate file plugins are deduped by exact resolved file spec. This happens while merging config, before plugin modules are loaded.
    35	- `plugin_enabled` is keyed by plugin id, not by plugin spec.
    36	- For file plugins, that id must come from the plugin module's exported `id`. For npm plugins, it is the exported `id` or the package name if `id` is omitted.
    37	- Plugins are enabled by default. `plugin_enabled` is only for explicit overrides, usually to disable a plugin with `false`.
    38	- `plugin_enabled` is merged across config layers.
    39	- Runtime enable/disable state is also stored in KV under `plugin_enabled`; that KV state overrides config on startup.
    40	
    41	## Author package shape
    42	
    43	Package entrypoint:
    44	
    45	- Import types from `@opencode-ai/plugin/tui`.
    46	- `@opencode-ai/plugin` exports `./tui` and declares optional peer deps on `@opentui/core` and `@opentui/solid`.
    47	
    48	Minimal module shape:
    49	
    50	```tsx
    51	/** @jsxImportSource @opentui/solid */
    52	import type { TuiPlugin, TuiPluginModule } from "@opencode-ai/plugin/tui"
    53	
    54	const tui: TuiPlugin = async (api, options, meta) => {
    55	  api.command.register(() => [
    56	    {
    57	      title: "Demo",
    58	      value: "demo.open",
    59	      onSelect: () => api.route.navigate("demo"),
    60	    },
    61	  ])
    62	
    63	  api.route.register([
    64	    {
    65	      name: "demo",
    66	      render: () => (
    67	        <box>
    68	          <text>demo</text>
    69	        </box>
    70	      ),
    71	    },
    72	  ])
    73	}
    74	
    75	const plugin: TuiPluginModule & { id: string } = {
    76	  id: "acme.demo",
    77	  tui,
    78	}
    79	
    80	export default plugin
    81	```
    82	
    83	- Loader only reads the module default export object. Named exports are ignored.
    84	- TUI shape is `default export { id?, tui }`; including `server` is rejected.
    85	- A single module cannot export both `server` and `tui`.
    86	- `tui` signature is `(api, options, meta) => Promise<void>`.
    87	- If package `exports` contains `./tui`, the loader resolves that entrypoint.
    88	- If package `exports` exists, loader only resolves `./tui` or `./server`; it never falls back to `exports["."]`.
    89	- For npm package specs, TUI does not use `package.json` `main` as a fallback entry.
    90	- `package.json` `main` is only used for server plugin entrypoint resolution.
    91	- If a package supports both server and TUI, use separate files and package `exports` (`./server` and `./tui`) so each target resolves to a target-only module.
    92	- File/path plugins must export a non-empty `id`.
    93	- npm plugins may omit `id`; package `name` is used.
    94	- Runtime identity is the resolved plugin id. Later plugins with the same id are rejected, including collisions with internal plugin ids.
    95	- If a path spec points at a directory, server loading can use `package.json` `main`.
    96	- TUI path loading never uses `package.json` `main`.
    97	- Legacy compatibility: path specs like `./plugin` can resolve to `./plugin/index.ts` (or `index.js`) when `package.json` is missing.
    98	- The `./plugin -> ./plugin/index.*` fallback applies to both server and TUI v1 loading.
    99	- There is no directory auto-discovery for TUI plugins; they must be listed in `tui.json`.
   100	
   101	## Package manifest and install
   102	
   103	Package manifest is read from `package.json` field `oc-plugin`.
   104	
   105	Example:
   106	
   107	```json
   108	{
   109	  "name": "@acme/opencode-plugin",
   110	  "type": "module",
   111	  "main": "./dist/index.js",
   112	  "engines": {
   113	    "opencode": "^1.0.0"
   114	  },
   115	  "oc-plugin": [
   116	    ["server", { "custom": true }],
   117	    ["tui", { "compact": true }]
   118	  ]
   119	}
   120	```
   121	
   122	### Version compatibility
   123	
   124	npm plugins can declare a version compatibility range in `package.json` using the standard `engines` field:
   125	
   126	```json
   127	{
   128	  "engines": {
   129	    "opencode": "^1.0.0"
   130	  }
   131	}
   132	```
   133	
   134	- The value is a semver range checked against the running OpenCode version.
   135	- If the range is not satisfied, the plugin is skipped with a warning and a session error.
   136	- If `engines.opencode` is absent, no check is performed (backward compatible).
   137	- File plugins are never checked; only npm package plugins are validated.
   138	
   139	- Install flow is shared by CLI and TUI in `src/plugin/install.ts`.
   140	- Shared helpers are `installPlugin`, `readPluginManifest`, and `patchPluginConfig`.
   141	- `opencode plugin <module>` and TUI install both run install → manifest read → config patch.
   142	- Alias: `opencode plug <module>`.
   143	- `-g` / `--global` writes into the global config dir.
   144	- Local installs resolve target dir inside `patchPluginConfig`.
   145	- For local scope, path is `<worktree>/.opencode` only when VCS is git and `worktree !== "/"`; otherwise `<directory>/.opencode`.
   146	- Root-worktree fallback (`worktree === "/"` uses `<directory>/.opencode`) is covered by regression tests.
   147	- `patchPluginConfig` applies all declared manifest targets (`server` and/or `tui`) in one call.
   148	- `patchPluginConfig` returns structured result unions (`ok`, `code`, fields by error kind) instead of custom thrown errors.
   149	- `patchPluginConfig` serializes per-target config writes with `Flock.acquire(...)`.
   150	- `patchPluginConfig` uses targeted `jsonc-parser` edits, so existing JSONC comments are preserved when plugin entries are added or replaced.
   151	- Without `--force`, an already-configured npm package name is a no-op.
   152	- With `--force`, replacement matches by package name. If the existing row is `[spec, options]`, those tuple options are kept.
   153	- Tuple targets in `oc-plugin` provide default options written into config.
   154	- A package can target `server`, `tui`, or both.
   155	- If a package targets both, each target must still resolve to a separate target-only module. Do not export `{ server, tui }` from one module.
   156	- There is no uninstall, list, or update CLI command for external plugins.
   157	- Local file plugins are configured directly in `tui.json`.
   158	
   159	When `plugin` entries exist in a writable `.opencode` dir or `OPENCODE_CONFIG_DIR`, OpenCode installs `@opencode-ai/plugin` into that dir and writes:
   160	
   161	- `package.json`
   162	- `bun.lock`
   163	- `node_modules/`
   164	- `.gitignore`
   165	
   166	That is what makes local config-scoped plugins able to import `@opencode-ai/plugin/tui`.
   167	
   168	## TUI plugin API
   169	
   170	Top-level API groups exposed to `tui(api, options, meta)`:
   171	
   172	- `api.app.version`
   173	- `api.command.register(cb)` / `api.command.trigger(value)`
   174	- `api.route.register(routes)` / `api.route.navigate(name, params?)` / `api.route.current`
   175	- `api.ui.Dialog`, `DialogAlert`, `DialogConfirm`, `DialogPrompt`, `DialogSelect`, `Prompt`, `ui.toast`, `ui.dialog`
   176	- `api.keybind.match`, `print`, `create`
   177	- `api.tuiConfig`
   178	- `api.kv.get`, `set`, `ready`
   179	- `api.state`
   180	- `api.theme.current`, `selected`, `has`, `set`, `install`, `mode`, `ready`
   181	- `api.client`, `api.scopedClient(workspaceID?)`, `api.workspace.current()`, `api.workspace.set(workspaceID?)`
   182	- `api.event.on(type, handler)`
   183	- `api.renderer`
   184	- `api.slots.register(plugin)`
   185	- `api.plugins.list()`, `activate(id)`, `deactivate(id)`, `add(spec)`, `install(spec, options?)`
   186	- `api.lifecycle.signal`, `api.lifecycle.onDispose(fn)`
   187	
   188	### Commands
   189	
   190	`api.command.register` returns an unregister function. Command rows support:
   191	
   192	- `title`, `value`
   193	- `description`, `category`
   194	- `keybind`
   195	- `suggested`, `hidden`, `enabled`
   196	- `slash: { name, aliases? }`
   197	- `onSelect`
   198	
   199	Command behavior:
   200	
   201	- Registrations are reactive.
   202	- Later registrations win for duplicate `value` and for keybind handling.
   203	- Hidden commands are removed from the command dialog and slash list, but still respond to keybinds and `command.trigger(value)` if `enabled !== false`.
   204	
   205	### Routes
   206	
   207	- Reserved route names: `home` and `session`.
   208	- Any other name is treated as a plugin route.
   209	- `api.route.current` returns one of:
   210	  - `{ name: "home" }`
   211	  - `{ name: "session", params: { sessionID, initialPrompt? } }`
   212	  - `{ name: string, params?: Record<string, unknown> }`
   213	- `api.route.navigate("session", params)` only uses `params.sessionID`. It cannot set `initialPrompt`.
   214	- If multiple plugins register the same route name, the last registered route wins.
   215	- Unknown plugin routes render a fallback screen with a `go home` action.
   216	
   217	### Dialogs and toast
   218	
   219	- `ui.Dialog` is the base dialog wrapper.
   220	- `ui.DialogAlert`, `ui.DialogConfirm`, `ui.DialogPrompt`, `ui.DialogSelect` are built-in dialog components.
   221	- `ui.Prompt` renders the same prompt component used by the host app.
   222	- `ui.toast(...)` shows a toast.
   223	- `ui.dialog` exposes the host dialog stack:
   224	  - `replace(render, onClose?)`
   225	  - `clear()`
   226	  - `setSize("medium" | "large" | "xlarge")`
   227	  - readonly `size`, `depth`, `open`
   228	
   229	### Keybinds
   230	
   231	- `api.keybind.match(key, evt)` and `print(key)` use the host keybind parser/printer.
   232	- `api.keybind.create(defaults, overrides?)` builds a plugin-local keybind set.
   233	- Only missing, blank, or non-string overrides are ignored. Key syntax is not validated.
   234	- Returned keybind set exposes `all`, `get(name)`, `match(name, evt)`, `print(name)`.
   235	
   236	### KV, state, client, events
   237	
   238	- `api.kv` is the shared app KV store backed by `state/kv.json`. It is not plugin-namespaced.
   239	- `api.kv` exposes `ready`.
   240	- `api.tuiConfig` and `api.state` are live host objects/getters, not frozen snapshots.
   241	- `api.state` exposes synced TUI state:
   242	  - `ready`
   243	  - `config`
   244	  - `provider`
   245	  - `path.{state,config,worktree,directory}`
   246	  - `vcs?.branch`
   247	  - `workspace.list()` / `workspace.get(workspaceID)`
   248	  - `session.count()`
   249	  - `session.diff(sessionID)`
   250	  - `session.todo(sessionID)`
   251	  - `session.messages(sessionID)`
   252	  - `session.status(sessionID)`
   253	  - `session.permission(sessionID)`
   254	  - `session.question(sessionID)`
   255	  - `part(messageID)`
   256	  - `lsp()`
   257	  - `mcp()`
   258	- `api.client` always reflects the current runtime client.
   259	- `api.scopedClient(workspaceID?)` creates or reuses a client bound to a workspace.
   260	- `api.workspace.set(...)` rebinds the active workspace; `api.client` follows that rebind.
   261	- `api.event.on(type, handler)` subscribes to the TUI event stream and returns an unsubscribe function.
   262	- `api.renderer` exposes the raw `CliRenderer`.
   263	
   264	### Theme
   265	
   266	- `api.theme.current` exposes the resolved current theme tokens.
   267	- `api.theme.selected` is the selected theme name.
   268	- `api.theme.has(name)` checks for an installed theme.
   269	- `api.theme.set(name)` switches theme and returns `boolean`.
   270	- `api.theme.mode()` returns `"dark" | "light"`.
   271	- `api.theme.install(jsonPath)` installs a theme JSON file.
   272	- `api.theme.ready` reports theme readiness.
   273	
   274	Theme install behavior:
   275	
   276	- Relative theme paths are resolved from the plugin root.
   277	- Theme name is the JSON basename.
   278	- First install writes only when the destination file is missing.
   279	- If the theme name already exists, install is skipped unless plugin metadata state is `updated`.
   280	- On `updated`, host only rewrites themes previously tracked for that plugin and only when source `mtime`/`size` changed.
   281	- Local plugins persist installed themes under the local `.opencode/themes` area near the plugin config source.
   282	- Global plugins persist installed themes under the global `themes` dir.
   283	- Invalid or unreadable theme files are ignored.
   284	
   285	### Slots
   286	
   287	Current host slot names:
   288	
   289	- `app`
   290	- `home_logo`
   291	- `home_prompt` with props `{ workspace_id? }`
   292	- `home_bottom`
   293	- `sidebar_title` with props `{ session_id, title, share_url? }`
   294	- `sidebar_content` with props `{ session_id }`
   295	- `sidebar_footer` with props `{ session_id }`
   296	
   297	Slot notes:
   298	
   299	- Slot context currently exposes only `theme`.
   300	- `api.slots.register(plugin)` returns the host-assigned slot plugin id.
   301	- `api.slots.register(plugin)` does not return an unregister function.
   302	- Returned ids are `pluginId`, `pluginId:1`, `pluginId:2`, and so on.
   303	- Plugin-provided `id` is not allowed.
   304	- The current host renders `home_logo` and `home_prompt` with `replace`, `sidebar_title` and `sidebar_footer` with `single_winner`, and `app`, `home_bottom`, and `sidebar_content` with the slot library default mode.
   305	- Plugins cannot define new slot names in this branch.
   306	
   307	### Plugin control and lifecycle
   308	
   309	- `api.plugins.list()` returns `{ id, source, spec, target, enabled, active }[]`.
   310	- `enabled` is the persisted desired state. `active` means the plugin is currently initialized.
   311	- `api.plugins.activate(id)` sets `enabled=true`, persists it into KV, and initializes the plugin.
   312	- `api.plugins.deactivate(id)` sets `enabled=false`, persists it into KV, and disposes the plugin scope.
   313	- `api.plugins.add(spec)` trims the input and returns `false` for an empty string.
   314	- `api.plugins.add(spec)` treats the input as the runtime plugin spec and loads it without re-reading `tui.json`.
   315	- `api.plugins.add(spec)` no-ops when that resolved spec (or resolved plugin id) is already loaded.
   316	- `api.plugins.add(spec)` assumes enabled and always attempts initialization (it does not consult config/KV enable state).
   317	- `api.plugins.install(spec, { global? })` runs install -> manifest read -> config patch using the same helper flow as CLI install.
   318	- `api.plugins.install(...)` returns either `{ ok: false, message, missing? }` or `{ ok: true, dir, tui }`.
   319	- `api.plugins.install(...)` does not load plugins into the current session. Call `api.plugins.add(spec)` to load after install.
